                "firstName": "W. W.",
                "lastName": "Lehman"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "A point raised by Mr. Price and also by Mr. Reid was the compounding of rectifiers, and it was considered that the plain shunt characteristic was preferable. In view of the fact that supply-voltage variations on the A.C. side are transmitted to the D.C. output voltage in the case of rectifiers, it is essential to have very good and reasonably equal voltage regulation for the sub-station supplies. These conditions are complied with in the case of concentrated suburban electrification schemes where the supply can be obtained from a stable well-regulated ring main or other suitable network. Under such conditions it might be desirable to consider rectifiers with a plain shunt characteristic which should, however, be as level as practicable to insure good voltage supply conditions under load, resulting in satisfactory acceleration of trains, which is one of the most important requirements of a suburban service.",
