                "firstName": "G. B.",
                "lastName": "Warren"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "In the first place, it is not true, as is generally stated, that the Port Washington Station is the most efficient in the world. There are two other staions which I believe are better. The first is the 1,200 lb. 825$dg F. initial 825$dg F. reheat 150,000 kW unit of the State Line Station, the performance of which was given in Fig 20 of my June 1940 A.S.M.E. paper, Mr Monks' reference No. 22. The performance of this turbine as compared with similar published performance of the Port Washington turbine will, I believe, show its superiority. So far as I know, no published figures have been made as to the overall performance of this section of the State Line power plant; and as normally operated it may not have as low a heat rate as is usually published for Port Washington because the Port Washington plant has been habitually operated at 55,000 kW its most economical load, and has averaged about 0$md5 in. absolute back pressure on account of running at less than full load with very cold cooling water throughout the year. Whereas, it is my understanding that this State Line unit, being by far the most efficient machine, even at its maximum load, on the system, has been run at nearer its maximum capacity, and the condensing water is not so cold.",
